{"step_index":47,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:39:31Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-23T13:39:31+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-23T13:39:32+07:00\nShowing lines 1-16 of 16\n1: Status: ready\n2: \n3: # Issue 02: Headless OpenAI & 9router AI Client\n4: \n5: ## Summary\n6: Implement a domain-agnostic, headless AI client in `src/core/ai.rs` compatible with OpenAI chat completion format (9router, OpenAI, DeepSeek, Ollama).\n7: \n8: ## Scope\n9: - Define `AiClient`, `ChatMessage`, `Role`, `CompletionRequest`, and `AiError`.\n10: - Implement `complete(prompt, system_prompt) -> Result<String, AiError>`.\n11: - Implement `complete_json_array(prompt, system_prompt) -> Result<Vec<String>, AiError>` with resilient fence stripping (handles ` ```json ... ``` `).\n12: - Write headless unit tests for JSON extraction and markdown fence handling.\n13: \n14: ## Acceptance Criteria\n15: - Pure core module with zero `egui` or GUI dependencies.\n16: - Unit tests verify extraction of JSON arrays from raw JSON strings, markdown-wrapped blocks, and strings with surrounding conversational text."}
{"step_index":48,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:39:31Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-23T13:39:31+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-23T13:39:32+07:00\nShowing lines 1-26 of 26\n1: # ADR 0002: Decoupled Generic AI Client (9router Compatible)\n2: \n3: ## Status\n4: Accepted\n5: \n6: ## Context\n7: Tools across Ryz will require language model interactions (e.g. subtitle translation, transcript summarization, code generation). Directly coupling AI client logic with subtitle lines or timestamps would prevent reuse and make testing cumbersome. Furthermore, the user uses **9router** (an AI proxy gateway) to handle provider fallbacks, rate-limiting, and routing.\n8: \n9: ## Decision\n10: We place the AI client in `src/core/ai.rs` as a completely domain-agnostic, headless module:\n11: \n12: 1. **Zero Domain Knowledge**: The AI client knows nothing about subtitles, timestamps, video, or files. It strictly consumes messages (`ChatMessage`) and returns completions.\n13: 2. **Dual Mode Completion**:\n14:    - `complete(prompt, system)`: Standard raw string completion.\n15:    - `complete_json_array(prompt, system)`: Structured array extraction.\n16: 3. **Resilient JSON Extraction**: Because LLMs through proxies often wrap JSON output in markdown backticks (` ```json [...] ``` `) or introductory commentary despite system prompts, the parser extracts JSON arrays safely using heuristic fence-stripping before deserialization.\n17: 4. **9router Ready**: The client uses standard OpenAI-compatible `/chat/completions` HTTP endpoints, accepting arbitrary `base_url`, `api_key`, and `model` configurations. Complex retry and backoff logic is kept minimal, relying on 9router.\n18: \n19: ## Consequences\n20: ### Positive\n21: - Any future tool can call `core::ai` without introducing subtitle or UI dependencies.\n22: - Can be tested headlessly with mock HTTP servers or test strings.\n23: - Compatible with all OpenAI-compatible servers (9router, Ollama, OpenRouter, DeepSeek, OpenAI).\n24: \n25: ### Negative\n26: - Tools must manage their own prompt formatting and map their domain structures into generic text messages."}
{"step_index":49,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:39:32Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-23T13:39:32+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-23T13:39:32+07:00\nShowing lines 1-33 of 33\n1: # Bounded Context: Ryz AI Studio\n2: \n3: ## Purpose\n4: Ryz is an extensible Rust desktop application for AI-assisted media workflows. Its core initial capability is the translation of subtitle (`.srt`) and plain text (`.txt`) documents, with an architectural foundation designed to host future media processing tools.\n5: \n6: ---\n7: \n8: ## Domain Glossary\n9: \n10: ### Core Domain: Tools & Application Shell\n11: - **Tool**: A self-contained functional capability implementing the `Tool` trait (`id`, `title`, `description`, `update`, `show`). Tools are dynamically dispatched by the main application shell (`RyzApp`).\n12: - **Tool Registry**: A collection of `Box<dyn Tool>` instances registered at application startup.\n13: - **Application Shell (`RyzApp`)**: The root `eframe` window and state container holding the active tool selection and the shared Tokio runtime.\n14: - **Sidebar**: The navigation panel allowing the user to collapse or switch between active tools.\n15: \n16: ### Core Domain: AI & Proxy\n17: - **AiClient**: A domain-agnostic HTTP client interfacing with OpenAI-compatible completion endpoints (specifically targeting the **9router** AI proxy gateway, as well as direct endpoints like OpenAI, Groq, DeepSeek, Ollama).\n18: - **CompletionRequest**: A request carrying a sequence of `ChatMessage` objects, model identifier, temperature, and optional JSON mode indicator.\n19: - **Resilient JSON Array**: An array payload extracted safely from LLM output, resilient to models wrapping arrays in markdown code fences (` ```json ... ``` `) or introductory text.\n20: \n21: ### Subtitle & Document Domain\n22: - **SubtitleFile**: An in-memory document model representing a parsed subtitle file with an optional file path and a list of `SubtitleLine` items.\n23: - **SubtitleLine**: A single subtitle block containing an index number, start timestamp string (`00:00:01,000`), end timestamp string (`00:00:04,000`), original dialogue text, and optional translated text.\n24: - **Duration ($T_{\\text{duration}}$)**: The active on-screen display duration of a subtitle line, calculated as $T_{\\text{end}} - T_{\\text{start}}$ in seconds.\n25: - **Characters Per Second (CPS)**: The reading speed density metric calculated as $\\frac{\\text{Length of Text}}{T_{\\text{duration}}}$.\n26: - **Duration Alert**: A warning triggered when $T_{\\text{duration}} < 1.0\\text{s}$ or $\\text{CPS} > 20$, surfaced in the UI with a warning icon (`⚠️`) and red color formatting (`Color32::from_rgb(240, 80, 80)`).\n27: - **Line Status**: The lifecycle state of a line during translation (`Pending`, `Translating`, `Done`, `Error`).\n28: \n29: ### Translation Pipeline Domain\n30: - **2-Phase Translation Pipeline**: The translation process dividing document processing into:\n31:   1. **Phase 1 (Global Context & Glossary Extraction)**: A preparatory pass where the AI analyzes the entire document or representative sample to summarize tone, setting, and extract a terminology glossary.\n32:   2. **Phase 2 (Sliding Batch Translation)**: Sequential translation of subtitle line batches (e.g. 20–25 lines) with the Phase 1 context and glossary injected into the system prompt.\n33: - **Translation Job**: An asynchronous background task managed via Tokio that communicates progress and results to the UI over non-blocking `std::sync::mpsc` channels without blocking the 60 FPS egui render loop."}
{"step_index":50,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:39:32Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-23T13:39:32+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-23T13:39:32+07:00\nShowing lines 1-121 of 121\n1: Status: ready\n2: \n3: # Specification: Extensible AI Subtitle & Text Translation Studio\n4: \n5: ## Problem Statement\n6: \n7: Content creators, video editors, and language learners frequently need to translate subtitle files (`.srt`) and transcript texts (`.txt`) across multiple languages while preserving dialogue timing, conversational context, and readability.\n8: \n9: Existing solutions present three critical friction points:\n10: 1. **Context Fragmentation & Pronoun Drift**: Translating subtitles line-by-line using standard machine translation or naive LLM prompts destroys conversational continuity. This results in inconsistent speaker names, gender/pronoun drift, and lost nuance across short subtitle turns.\n11: 2. **Timing & Reading Speed Violations**: Target languages often require significantly more syllables or characters than the original dialogue. Subtitles that stay on screen for too short a time ($< 1.0\\text{s}$) or exceed human reading speed ($> 20\\text{ characters per second}$) cause severe viewer fatigue, but creators have no visual indication when lines violate these limits.\n12: 3. **Rigid Application Architecture**: Dedicated subtitle translation scripts cannot be extended into a broader multimedia tool suite (such as audio transcribers or video editors) without completely re-architecting the application.\n13: \n14: ## Solution\n15: \n16: Ryz AI Studio provides a native desktop GUI built on an extensible tool architecture. Its core Subtitle & Text Translation Tool provides:\n17: - First-class ingestion and export for both `.srt` and `.txt` documents.\n18: - A **2-Phase Translation Pipeline** interfacing with an OpenAI-compatible proxy gateway (such as 9router):\n19:   - **Phase 1**: An initial pass that extracts global context, tone, and a terminology glossary across the entire document.\n20:   - **Phase 2**: Sequential sliding batch translation with the Phase 1 glossary and context injected into each prompt, returning strict JSON arrays.\n21: - A virtualized inspection table presenting indices, timestamps, duration, original text, editable translated text, and row status.\n22: - Real-time **Duration Alerts** and **Reading Speed Warnings** that flag subtitles that are too short ($< 1.0\\text{s}$) or too dense ($> 20\\text{ CPS}$) with a visual warning icon and red highlight.\n23: - An extensible **Tool** lifecycle architecture driven by a background async runtime, ensuring the 60 FPS user interface never stutters during network requests.\n24: \n25: ## User Stories\n26: \n27: 1. As a video creator, I want to load an `.srt` subtitle file into the application, so that I can view all timestamps, durations, and original dialogue lines in a structured table.\n28: 2. As a content translator, I want to load a plain `.txt` file, so that I can translate dialogue scripts and articles without needing timing data.\n29: 3. As a user, I want to open the Settings tool to configure my AI proxy base URL (e.g., 9router), API key, and default model name, so that all tools share my preferred LLM provider.\n30: 4. As a user, I want my API credentials masked in the Settings interface, so that sensitive keys are not exposed to onlookers.\n31: 5. As a user, I want to select a target language (e.g., Spanish, Indonesian, Japanese) from the Subtitle Tool header, so that translations match my desired audience.\n32: 6. As a subtitler, I want the translation engine to run a Phase 1 context analysis, so that character names, gender references, and domain terminology are identified upfront.\n33: 7. As a subtitler, I want the Phase 1 extracted glossary to be injected into subsequent translation prompts, so that terminology remains consistent across the entire video.\n34: 8. As a subtitler, I want dialogue lines to be translated in sliding batches of 20 to 25 lines, so that the LLM maintains conversational flow without exceeding output token limits.\n35: 9. As a developer, I want the AI client to enforce strict JSON array output and automatically strip accidental markdown code blocks, so that formatting variations from different models or proxies never fail the translation job.\n36: 10. As a user, I want translation tasks to execute on a background async runtime, so that the user interface remains fluid and responsive at 60 FPS.\n37: 11. As a user, I want each row in the subtitle table to display a live status badge (`Pending`, `Translating`, `Done`, `Error`), so that I can track translation progress in real time.\n38: 12. As a user, I want an active progress bar with a cancel button, so that I can stop a job immediately if I notice misconfigured settings.\n39: 13. As a video editor, I want subtitle lines with a duration shorter than 1.0 second to be highlighted with a red alert and a warning icon, so that I can identify lines that flash too quickly on screen.\n40: 14. As a video editor, I want subtitle lines with a reading speed exceeding 20 characters per second to trigger a red alert, so that I can detect text that viewers cannot comfortably read.\n41: 15. As a translator, I want to click into any translated text cell and edit it inline, so that I can correct translation errors or rephrase lines before exporting.\n42: 16. As a user, I want to export the translated content back to a clean `.srt` file preserving original timestamps and sequence numbers, so that it is ready for video players or video editing software.\n43: 17. As a user, I want to export translated plain text to a `.txt` file, so that I can use the translated script in other applications.\n44: 18. As an app user, I want a collapsible sidebar navigation, so that I can switch between tools or collapse the menu to maximize table editing space.\n45: 19. As a developer, I want all core domain models (AI client, SRT parser, TXT parser) to be strictly headless with zero GUI dependencies, so that they can be tested and reused without spinning up a window.\n46: 20. As a developer, I want new features to be added by implementing an independent `Tool` trait, so that future tools (transcription, audio processing) can be integrated without modifying existing tool code.\n47: \n48: ## Implementation Decisions\n49: \n50: ### 1. Modular Decoupling & UI Boundary\n51: - **Headless Core**: All parsing, duration calculation, and AI interaction logic are strictly headless, holding zero GUI dependencies.\n52: - **Dedicated UI Layer**: All layout, table construction, widget rendering, inline text editors, and color styling live exclusively in the presentation layer.\n53: - **Granularity Strategy**: Modules with a single responsibility remain in single files. Complex tools with multiple distinct roles (orchestration, state machine, background async worker) are grouped into a dedicated tool directory.\n54: \n55: ### 2. Extensible Tool Architecture\n56: - Interactive tools implement a dynamic trait:\n57:   ```rust\n58:   // Encodes the tool lifecycle decision\n59:   pub trait Tool: Send + 'static {\n60:       fn id(&self) -> &'static str;\n61:       fn title(&self) -> &str;\n62:       fn description(&self) -> &str;\n63:       fn update(&mut self, ctx: &eframe::egui::Context);\n64:       fn show(&mut self, ctx: &eframe::egui::Context, ui: &mut eframe::egui::Ui);\n65:   }\n66:   ```\n67: - The application shell manages a polymorphic collection of tools and delegates rendering to the active tool.\n68: \n69: ### 3. Headless AI Client & 9router Compatibility\n70: - The AI client is completely domain-agnostic; it has no concept of subtitles, timestamps, or documents.\n71: - Uses standard OpenAI-compatible `/chat/completions` HTTP endpoints.\n72: - Provides standard text completions and structured JSON array extractions.\n73: - Implements resilient parsing that strips markdown code fences (` ```json ... ``` `) and conversational preamble before deserializing JSON arrays.\n74: - Relies on the 9router proxy for provider fallbacks and retries, keeping client-side error handling simple and explicit.\n75: \n76: ### 4. 2-Phase Subtitle Translation Pipeline\n77: - **Phase 1 (Global Context & Glossary Extraction)**: A document summary and key terminology glossary are generated by analyzing the document overview.\n78: - **Phase 2 (Sliding Batch Translation)**: Lines are processed in chunks of 20–25 lines. The Phase 1 context and glossary are injected into the system prompt. The model responds with a strict JSON array of translated strings matching the chunk length.\n79: - If a batch response count mismatches the input count, the batch is marked as an error rather than risking timestamp misalignment.\n80: \n81: ### 5. Duration & Reading Speed Alerts\n82: - Subtitle duration is calculated as $T_{\\text{end}} - T_{\\text{start}}$ in seconds.\n83: - Reading speed is calculated as $\\text{CPS} = \\frac{\\text{Length of Text}}{T_{\\text{duration}}}$.\n84: - A Duration Alert is triggered when $T_{\\text{duration}} < 1.0\\text{s}$ or $\\text{CPS} > 20$.\n85: - Alerted cells display a warning icon (`⚠️`) and red color styling.\n86: \n87: ### 6. Asynchronous Concurrency\n88: - Network requests and translation jobs run on a background async runtime.\n89: - Background tasks communicate with the main thread through non-blocking message channels (`Command` $\\rightarrow$ Worker, `Event` $\\rightarrow$ UI).\n90: \n91: ## Testing Decisions\n92: \n93: ### What Makes a Good Test\n94: Tests must verify external behavioral contracts, calculation correctness, and error resilience without touching GUI internals or creating windowing contexts.\n95: \n96: ### Tested Modules & Seams\n97: 1. **Primary Seam: Subtitle Translation State Reducer**\n98:    - Verify that incoming translation batch events correctly update target line translations and lifecycle status flags without index drift.\n99: 2. **Seam: Subtitle Parsing & Readability Calculations**\n100:    - Verify parsing of standard SRT, CRLF line endings, BOM markers, multi-line dialogue blocks, and empty inputs.\n101:    - Verify timestamp conversion into duration seconds.\n102:    - Verify alert threshold logic ($< 1.0\\text{s}$ and $> 20\\text{ CPS}$).\n103:    - Verify serialization preserves formatting and timestamps.\n104: 3. **Seam: Plain Text Document Parser**\n105:    - Verify text line chunking, blank line handling, and serialization.\n106: 4. **Seam: AI Client Resilient JSON Array Parser**\n107:    - Verify extraction of JSON arrays from raw JSON, markdown-wrapped blocks, and strings with conversational text.\n108: \n109: ### Prior Art\n110: - Baseline testing conventions follow the existing unit tests in the subtitle parser test suite (`test_parse_standard_srt`, `test_crlf_and_bom`, `test_empty_content`, `test_serialize_srt`).\n111: \n112: ## Out of Scope\n113: \n114: - Embedded audio/video player playback.\n115: - Direct audio extraction via embedded ffmpeg binaries.\n116: - Speech-to-text / automatic audio transcription (Whisper), reserved as a future distinct tool.\n117: - Local offline neural machine translation models (candle, ONNX).\n118: \n119: ## Further Notes\n120: \n121: - Configuration credentials (Base URL, API Key, Model) are managed through the Settings tool and persisted locally."}
